# Behavioural tests: Tiger programs, compiled by us, run, and checked
# against their expected stdout and exit code.
#
# Translated from tiger.x86.tst, which was Lua. The Lua driver is gone but
# the data is worth keeping: these expectations are upstream's, and they
# have already earned their keep - "hello rc=14" is how we confirmed our
# own exit code was correct rather than a bug.
#
# Dropped from the original: the .s/.s1/.s2 assembly comparisons, which
# cannot pass here. We use a different register allocator from upstream
# (flowra, not dls or color) and run no optimizer, so our generated code
# legitimately differs from the checked-in tiger/x86/*.s everywhere.
#
# Columns:  name  source  expected-rc  stdin ("-" for none, else a name
#           looked up in tiger/input/)
# Expected stdout is always tiger/output/<name>.1

arrays      arrays.c--        1    -
colmajor    colmajor.c--      0    colmajor.0
exceptions  exceptions.c--    18   -
forloop     forloop.c--       1    -
funcall     funcall.c--       1    -
hello       hello.c--         14   -
merge       merge.c--         1    merge.0
qsort       qsort.c--         0    -
queens      queens.c--        0    -
rc4         rc4.c--           215  rc4.0
rb          rb.c--            0    wf.0
rbshort     rb.c--            0    rbshort.0
sieve       sieve.c--         0    -
wf          wf.c--            0    wf.0
wff         wff.c--           0    wf.0
